## Summary

The database groundwork for the Operations Admin story (CS455-46): roles, the audit log, fares, complaints, warnings, suspensions and the AI tables, and the shared rules both of us build the admin work on.

> First of three PRs for the admin work. Merge in order: this one (CS455-47), then CS455-48, then CS455-49. Each targets master; the later ones also show the earlier commits until those merge.

### Changes

- **`db/schema.sql`** (appended; like the rest of the file it reruns cleanly on every start):
  - `users.role`: `student` or `admin`, default `student`;
  - `admin_audit_log`: who acted (an admin, the operator script or the system), the action, what it was about, the reason, the state before and after (JSON), and the outcome (`succeeded`, `refused` or `failed`). A trigger refuses UPDATE and DELETE, so it is append-only;
  - a `version` column on `locations` and `vehicle_types`, so two admins editing the same item cannot overwrite each other;
  - `campus_fares` (one fare per pair of campus places and vehicle type) and `external_fare_ranges` (lowest and highest fare per hub and vehicle type);
  - `complaints`:
    - reference `CMP-` plus 8 characters; the rider categories Safety, Harassment, Tardiness, Payment, No-show and Other; 20 to 2000 characters from the form; status Submitted, Under review or Resolved, and whether action was taken;
    - one complaint per complainant, ride and accused, never about yourself, and both people must have a seat on the ride;
    - a trigger stops anyone editing the original text, category or people;
  - `complaint_chat_messages`: the chat messages a complaint is about;
  - `rider_warnings`;
  - `rider_suspensions`:
    - a suspension has an end time or is indefinite, never both and never neither;
    - lifting it needs a reason and happens once;
    - the `active_rider_suspensions` view means a suspension ends by itself at its end time, with no job needed;
  - `complaint_ai_analyses` and `ai_enforcement_decisions`:
    - anything other than "approved" needs a reason;
    - a rejection takes no action;
    - the action taken must match the linked warning or suspension;
  - a trigger refuses a warning, suspension, lift or AI decision when the person recorded as making it is not an admin.
- **Chat reports become complaints, as agreed:**
  - existing `ride_chat_complaints` rows are copied into `complaints` (`source = chat_report`);
  - a trigger copies each new report until the report flow writes complaints itself (A6);
  - several reports about one person on one ride join one complaint.
- **`scripts/admin-role.mjs`:**
  - `npm run admin:grant -- name@iitk.ac.in` and `npm run admin:revoke -- name@iitk.ac.in`;
  - works only on verified accounts, and each change is audited as an operator action;
  - nothing in the app can change a role.
- **Shared rules** (pure functions, so the pages and the APIs check the same way):
  - `complaint-rules.ts`: categories and labels, the 7-day filing window, the limits, `parseComplaintRequest()`;
  - `admin-config-rules.ts`: names of 2 to 40 characters, capacity 1 to 12, fares 1 to 50,000, lowest fare no higher than highest, and a parser for every configuration body;
  - `admin-types.ts`: the request and response shapes of the admin APIs, as in the contract we agreed;
  - `suspensions.ts`: `getActiveSuspension(userId)`.
- **For Mahir, your parts (all additions):**
  - the `notifications.type` check now also allows `account_warning`, `account_suspended`, `account_reactivated` and `complaint_update`. The constraint is dropped and re-added only when it lacks them;
  - a new AFTER INSERT trigger on `ride_chat_complaints` (`ride_chat_complaints_mirror`). If it fails it only logs a warning, so a chat report never fails because of it. `ride_chat_complaints` itself is unchanged.
- **CI and docs:**
  - `.github/workflows/docker.yml` also checks that `admin_audit_log` and `complaints` exist after start;
  - README;
  - setup.md "Making an operations admin".

